.Dd October 5, 2026
.Dt LW 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm lw
.Nd LogWisp log transport
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl t | Fl \-dump
.Op Fl q
.Op Fl c Ar file
.Op Fl \-color Op Ar when
.Op Fl \- Ns Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value ...
.Op Fl p Ar spec
.Op Fl \-pipeline Ar name
.Op Fl \-source Ar spec ...
.Op Fl \-sink Ar spec ...
.Op Fl \-filter Ar spec ...
.Op Fl \-format Ar spec
.Op Fl \-rate\-limit Ar spec
.Op Fl \-heartbeat Ar spec
.Nm
.Cm auth | tls | preset | config
.Ar command
.Op Ar flags
.Nm
.Fl V | Fl \-version
.Nm
.Cm help | Fl h | Fl \-help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs LogWisp: independent pipelines, each reading entries from sources,
passing them through a flow of rate limiting, filters and formatting, and
writing them to sinks.
Sources tail files, read standard input or accept other LogWisp nodes;
sinks write to the console or files, serve live streams over HTTP or TCP, or
forward to other nodes.
Every network source and sink can use TLS, mutual TLS and
Argon2id-SCRAM password authentication.
.Pp
With no configuration file and no pipeline options,
.Nm
is a filter like
.Xr cat 1 :
it copies standard input to standard output line by line and exits 0 at the
end of input.
This built-in pipeline,
.Dq pipe ,
joins the console source
.Dq stdin
to the console sink
.Dq stdout
in the raw format, without a filter or rate limit; a configuration file that
defines no pipelines runs it too.
Each line is written with one newline, whether it ended in a newline, a
carriage return and newline, or nothing; blank lines are skipped, and a line
over 1 MiB continues in the next one.
When standard input is a terminal,
.Nm
prints a reminder on standard error that Control-D ends the input.
.Pp
A pipeline finishes when all its sources have ended.
Only a console source ends, at the end of standard input; every other
source runs until shutdown.
When every pipeline has finished,
.Nm
shuts down gracefully and exits 0.
Every sink first writes what it holds; the
.Cm http
and
.Cm tcp
sinks give each client
.Cm write_timeout_ms ,
at most 2 seconds, to take it.
Under a supervisor, standard input is usually
.Pa /dev/null :
a console source there ends at once.
.Pp
The console source and sink never drop a line.
A busy pipeline slows the reading of standard input; a console sink whose
output is slow makes its pipeline wait, other sinks included.
On a terminal the console sink writes control characters, escape sequences
included, as the hex of their bytes in angle brackets, such as
.Ql <1b> ,
so that a log line cannot drive the terminal; pipes and files get the bytes
unchanged.
Its
.Cm escape
option sets this:
.Cm auto
.Pq the default ,
.Cm always
or
.Cm never .
Its
.Cm color
option, by default the top-level
.Cm color ,
paints the level name in each line: debug cyan, info green, warn yellow,
error bold red, trace grey, in the 16 colors every terminal and text console
renders, with the terminal theme choosing the shades.
.Pp
.Nm
stays in the foreground and logs to standard error; run a configuration as a
service under a supervisor such as
.Xr systemd 1
or
.Xr daemon 8 .
.Pp
Every option has a long form, and the frequent ones a one-letter short form
that stands for it in every command that has it.
A value follows as the next argument or after
.Ql = ;
a value that starts with
.Ql - ,
and a switch's, follows
.Ql = .
Short options do not combine.
.Nm
takes options only: an unknown option, or a word no option takes, is a usage
error.
The options of
.Nm
join words with
.Ql - ;
a configuration or preset key keeps its TOML spelling.
The commands also take a long option after a single dash.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c Ar file , Fl \-config Ar file , Fl \-config= Ns Ar file
Read the configuration from
.Ar file ,
which must exist.
The last one given wins.
.It Fl t , Fl \-check
Build every pipeline and plugin as a start would, loading options, TLS files
and credentials files and printing startup warnings, without binding a port
or opening, reading or creating a log file; print
.Dq configuration ok
and exit 0, or the error and exit 1, or 2 when a named configuration file is
missing
.Pq see Sx EXIT STATUS .
.It Fl \-dump
Print the effective configuration, with the options, environment and
presets resolved into it, as a file
.Fl c
reads back, and exit.
.It Fl q , Fl \-quiet
Turn off the log of
.Nm
and the terminal reminder; console sinks still write.
.It Fl \-color Op Ar when
Color the level names console sinks write:
.Cm auto
.Pq the default: on a terminal, with Ev NO_COLOR unset and Ev TERM not Cm dumb ,
.Cm always
.Pq what a bare Fl \-color No means
or
.Cm never .
A console sink's own
.Cm color
option takes precedence.
The codes go into whatever the format makes, JSON included, so output another
program parses keeps
.Cm auto
or
.Cm never .
.It Fl V , Fl \-version
Print the version, commit and build time, and exit.
.It Fl h , Fl \-help , Cm help
Print a summary of every option and exit.
.It Fl \-
End option processing.
.El
.Ss Configuration keys
Every scalar configuration key is an option by its TOML path,
.Fl \- Ns Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
or
.Fl \- Ns Ar key Ar value ,
such as
.Fl \-logging.level=debug ;
a bare
.Fl \- Ns Ar key
sets a boolean to true.
Besides the options above, the keys are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl \-status_reporter= Ns Ar bool
Log pipeline statistics every 30 seconds at the debug level
.Pq default true with a configuration file, false without .
.It Fl \-auto_reload= Ns Ar bool
Reload when the configuration file changes
.Pq default false .
.It Fl \-logging.output= Ns Ar mode
Where the log of
.Nm
itself goes:
.Cm stderr
.Pq the default ,
.Cm stdout ,
.Cm split
.Pq warnings and errors to standard error, the rest to standard output ,
.Cm file ,
.Cm all
.Pq split and file
or
.Cm none .
The modes that use standard output mix the log into what a console sink
writes there.
.It Fl \-logging.level= Ns Ar level
One of
.Cm debug , info , warn
or
.Cm error ;
the default is
.Cm info
with a configuration file and
.Cm warn
without.
.It Fl \-logging.format= Ns Ar format
One of
.Cm txt
.Pq the default ,
.Cm json
or
.Cm raw .
.It Fl \-logging.sanitization= Ns Ar policy
How the log's text is cleaned:
.Cm raw
.Pq the default: unchanged ,
.Cm txt
.Pq Ql < No and non-printable characters as hex ,
.Cm json
.Pq control characters escaped
or
.Cm shell
.Pq shell metacharacters, whitespace and control characters removed; lossy .
.It Fl \-logging.file.directory= Ns Ar dir
The directory the
.Cm file
and
.Cm all
modes write in
.Pq default Pa ./log .
It must be
.Nm Ns 's
alone: the limits below count and delete every
.Pa .log
file in it but the one being written, and old ones are also deleted when the
disk has less than 10 MB free.
.It Fl \-logging.file.name= Ns Ar name
The log file's name before
.Pa .log
.Pq default Cm logwisp ;
see
.Sx FILES .
.It Fl \-logging.file.max_size_mb= Ns Ar n
Start a new file when the current one would pass
.Ar n
megabytes
.Pq default 100, 0 for no limit .
.It Fl \-logging.file.max_total_size_mb= Ns Ar n
Delete the oldest
.Pa .log
files in the directory when all of them pass
.Ar n
megabytes
.Pq default 1000, 0 for no limit .
.It Fl \-logging.file.retention_hours= Ns Ar hours
Delete the
.Pa .log
files in the directory older than
.Ar hours
.Pq default 168, 0 to keep them .
.It Fl \-logging.console.target= Ns Ar target
Accepted, as
.Cm stdout ,
.Cm stderr
or
.Cm split ,
and ignored:
.Fl \-logging.output
chooses the stream.
.El
.Ss Pipeline specifications
Pipelines can be given on the command line or in the environment instead of
the configuration file.
They then replace the file's pipelines and the built-in default; every other
key keeps its usual precedence.
A reload rereads the file but keeps these pipelines.
A pipeline without a source reads standard input with the console source
.Dq stdin ;
one without a sink writes standard output with the console sink
.Dq stdout .
Standard input has one reader: a second console source anywhere in the
configuration fails validation.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl \-pipeline Ar name
Start a pipeline named
.Ar name ,
which the pipeline options after it configure.
Those before any
.Fl \-pipeline ,
and the variables without
.Ev LOGWISP_PIPELINE ,
make a pipeline named after its preset, or
.Dq cli
without one.
.It Fl p Ar spec , Fl \-preset Ar spec
Start the pipeline with a preset, described in
.Sx Presets ;
later options add sources, sinks and filters to it, or replace its format.
.It Fl \-source Ar spec , Fl \-sink Ar spec , Fl \-filter Ar spec
Add a source, sink or filter; each is repeatable.
.It Fl \-format Ar spec , Fl \-rate\-limit Ar spec , Fl \-heartbeat Ar spec
Set the pipeline's formatter, rate limit and heartbeat.
.El
.Pp
A source, sink, filter or format
.Ar spec
is a type followed by comma-separated
.Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
pairs; a rate limit or heartbeat
.Ar spec
has the pairs only:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\-\-source file,directory=/var/log/app,pattern=*.log
\-\-sink http,host=127.0.0.1,port=8080,id=web
\-\-filter include,patterns=ERROR,patterns=WARN
\-\-format json
\-\-rate\-limit rate=100,burst=200,policy=drop
.Ed
.Pp
Keys are dotted paths into the plugin's options, such as
.Cm tls.cert_file .
A repeated key makes a list.
A backslash escapes
.Ql \&, ,
.Ql =
and
.Ql \e
inside a value; quote such a
.Ar spec
so the shell keeps the backslash.
.Cm id= Ns Ar name
names a source or sink; the default is its type, then
.Ar type Ns _2 ,
.Ar type Ns _3
and so on.
Any pipeline flag on the command line makes
.Nm
ignore the pipeline variables in
.Sx ENVIRONMENT .
.Ss Presets
A preset builds a common pipeline from a few keys:
.Fl \-preset Ar name Ns Op , Ns Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value ... ,
in the syntax of the other specifications;
.Nm
.Cm preset Ar name Op Fl \- Ns Ar key Ar value ...
prints the pipeline it expands to, and
.Fl h
there lists its keys and defaults; its flags are the keys, spelled as in the
specification, and
.Fl u
is
.Fl \-user .
A
.Ar path
is a file, a directory, whose files are followed, or a glob; without one, a
preset that takes it reads standard input.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm pipe Op Cm format
Standard input to standard output, the pipeline
.Nm
runs without a configuration.
.It Cm tail path Op Cm from , format
Follow files to standard output, like
.Ql tail \-F .
.It Cm serve Oo Cm path , listen , format , tls , users , proxy , viewer , allow , deny Oc
Serve a live HTTP stream on
.Cm listen
.Pq default Li 127.0.0.1:8080 ,
each stream starting with the last 1000 entries.
.Cm tls
is
.Cm off ,
.Cm self
.Pq a self-signed certificate made at startup ,
.Cm issuer
.Pq one signed by Cm issuer_cert No and Cm issuer_key
or
.Cm files
.Pq Cm cert No and Cm key ;
.Cm users
names a credentials file, so readers log in with SCRAM.
A browser at the root gets a live viewer; with
.Cm users ,
only behind a TLS-terminating proxy named by
.Cm proxy ,
with
.Cm viewer=true ,
since a browser cannot bind its login to the TLS channel.
.Cm allow
and
.Cm deny
list the addresses or CIDRs the listener admits and refuses.
.It Cm edge to Oo Cm path , transport , ca | pin , user , password_file , cert , key , node Oc
Forward to an aggregator at
.Cm to
over TLS, by
.Cm tcp_chain
or, with
.Cm transport=http ,
.Cm http_chain .
It refuses to run without credentials:
.Cm user
and
.Cm password_file ,
or a client
.Cm cert
and
.Cm key .
.It Cm aggregator Oo Cm listen , transport , tls , users , client_ca , out , format , allow , deny Oc
Receive from edges on
.Cm listen
.Pq default Li 0.0.0.0:9000
over TLS, never off
.Pq default Cm tls=self ,
authenticated by the
.Cm users
credentials file or the client certificates
.Cm client_ca
vouches for, and write to standard output or to files in
.Cm out ;
.Cm allow
and
.Cm deny
as for
.Cm serve .
.El
.Ss Certificate commands
.Nm
.Cm tls
makes ECDSA P-256 certificates; it never replaces a file, and writes keys
with mode 0600.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm ca Fl \-dir Ar dir Oo Fl \-name Ar name Oc Op Fl \-days Ar n
Create a certificate authority,
.Pa dir/ca.crt
and
.Pa dir/ca.key ,
valid 3650 days.
.It Cm cert Fl \-ca\-dir Ar dir Fl \-name Ar name Oo Fl \-hosts Ar names Oc Oo Fl \-server Oc Oo Fl \-client Oc Oo Fl \-days Ar n Oc Op Fl \-out Ar dir
Issue
.Pa name.crt
and
.Pa name.key
from that authority, valid 397 days and never past it.
.Fl \-hosts
.Pq or Fl \-host
lists the DNS names and addresses a server certificate carries
.Pq default: Ar name ;
.Ar name
is also the subject, the identity
.Cm auth.type=mtls
checks.
.El
.Pp
A listener needs no files:
.Cm tls.self_signed=true ,
or
.Cm tls.issuer_cert_file
with
.Cm tls.issuer_key_file ,
make its certificate at startup, for
.Cm tls.hosts ,
its
.Cm host ,
this machine's name, localhost and the loopback addresses.
Its key lasts until
.Nm
exits, across reloads.
A self-signed listener logs the
.Cm tls.pin_sha256
that dialers set in place of
.Cm tls.ca_file ;
it changes when
.Nm
restarts, so a fleet uses an issuer instead.
.Ss Authentication commands
.Nm
.Cm auth
manages SCRAM credentials files and logs in to SCRAM listeners.
.Fl u
is short for
.Fl \-user .
Changes to a credentials file apply when the server receives
.Dv SIGHUP .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm add-user Fl \-credentials Ar file Fl u Ar name Oo Fl \-password\-file Ar file Oc Op Fl \-generate
Add a user, or replace its password.
The password is read from the
.Fl \-password\-file
when it exists; otherwise one is generated and written there, or printed
once to standard output.
.Fl \-generate
replaces an existing password without reading one.
A missing or empty credentials file is created with mode 0600; an existing
one keeps its owner and mode.
.It Cm remove-user Fl \-credentials Ar file Fl u Ar name
Remove a user.
The last user cannot be removed.
.It Cm token Fl \-url Ar https://host:port Fl u Ar name Fl \-password\-file Ar file Oo Ar tls-flags Oc Op Fl \-unbound
Log in to an
.Cm http
sink or
.Cm http_chain
source and print a bearer token.
.Fl \-unbound
logs in without channel binding, to an
.Cm http
sink behind a TLS-terminating proxy; the URL may then carry the proxy's path.
.It Cm stream Fl \-addr Ar host:port Fl u Ar name Fl \-password\-file Ar file Op Ar tls-flags
Log in to a
.Cm tcp
sink and copy its stream to standard output until interrupted.
.El
.Pp
The
.Ar tls-flags
are
.Fl \-ca\-file Ar file
.Pq default: the system roots
or
.Fl \-pin\-sha256 Ar pin ,
.Fl \-server\-name Ar name ,
and
.Fl \-cert\-file Ar file
with
.Fl \-key\-file Ar file
for a listener that requires client certificates.
There is no flag to skip server verification.
An IPv6
.Ar host
is written in brackets, as in
.Li [2001:db8::1]:8443 .
.Ss Configuration command
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm config init Op Fl \-out Ar file
Write the annotated configuration
.Nm
ships, every option with its default, to
.Ar file ,
by default
.Pa ~/.config/logwisp/logwisp.toml ,
the first file in
.Sx FILES .
An existing file is never replaced.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev LOGWISP_ Ns Ar KEY
Any scalar configuration key: its TOML path in upper case with
.Ql \&.
replaced by
.Ql _ ,
as in
.Ev LOGWISP_LOGGING_LEVEL=debug .
Command-line options take precedence, and both take precedence over the
configuration file.
.It Ev LOGWISP_CONFIG_FILE
The configuration file, relative to
.Ev LOGWISP_CONFIG_DIR
when both are set.
.It Ev LOGWISP_CONFIG_DIR
The directory holding
.Pa logwisp.toml .
.It Ev LOGWISP_PIPELINE
The name of the one pipeline the variables below define.
.It Ev LOGWISP_PRESET
The preset that starts it.
All of these are ignored when a pipeline option is on the command line.
.It Ev LOGWISP_SOURCE , LOGWISP_SINK , LOGWISP_FILTER
Source, sink and filter specifications, in the syntax of the matching
options; a suffix
.Ar _N ,
counting from 1, adds more, as in
.Ev LOGWISP_SINK_1 .
.It Ev LOGWISP_FORMAT , LOGWISP_RATE_LIMIT , LOGWISP_HEARTBEAT
The pipeline's format, rate limit and heartbeat specifications.
.It Ev NO_COLOR , TERM
Set to anything,
.Ev NO_COLOR
turns
.Cm auto
color off, as does
.Ev TERM=dumb .
.It Ev HOME
Where
.Pa ~/.config/logwisp/logwisp.toml
is looked for.
.El
.Pp
Secrets never go in the environment: TLS keys, credentials files and
password files are named by path
.Pq Cm tls.key_file , auth.credentials_file , auth.password_file .
.Sh FILES
Without
.Fl c
or the
.Ev LOGWISP_CONFIG_*
variables,
.Nm
reads the first of these that exists:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
.Pa ~/.config/logwisp/logwisp.toml
.It
.Pa ./logwisp.toml
.El
.Pp
.Nm
.Cm config init
writes the first.
A service installation places the annotated configuration, keeping one
already there, and service files that pass it with
.Fl c :
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/logwisp/logwisp.toml
The configuration on Linux, used by
.Pa logwisp.service .
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/logwisp/logwisp.toml
The configuration on
.Fx ,
used by
.Pa /usr/local/etc/rc.d/logwisp .
.It Pa /var/lib/logwisp , /var/db/logwisp
The service's working directory on Linux and on
.Fx .
.It Pa ./log/logwisp.log
The log of
.Nm
itself with
.Fl \-logging.output=file
or
.Cm all ,
in
.Fl \-logging.file.directory
and named by
.Fl \-logging.file.name ;
a full one is renamed with its time, as
.Pa logwisp_YYMMDD_HHMMSS.log .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 at the end of input, after a clean shutdown, and for
.Fl \-version ,
help,
.Fl \-dump
and a configuration
.Fl \-check
accepts; 1 on a failure, such as a configuration that does not load, or a
logger or startup error; and 2 on a usage error or when a configuration file
named by
.Fl c
or
.Ev LOGWISP_CONFIG_*
does not exist.
A reader of standard output that goes away, as in
.Ql lw | head ,
ends
.Nm
with
.Dv SIGPIPE ,
as it ends
.Xr cat 1 ;
the shell reports status 141.
.Pp
.Nm
.Cm auth ,
.Cm tls ,
.Cm preset
and
.Cm config
do the same: 0 on success or help, 1 on failure, and 2 on a usage error.
.Sh SIGNALS
.Bl -tag -width "SIGINT, SIGTERM"
.It Dv SIGINT , SIGTERM
Shut down gracefully.
.It Dv SIGHUP , SIGUSR1
Reread the configuration file, certificates and credentials, and rebuild
every pipeline.
An invalid configuration leaves the running service untouched.
Before the service runs,
.Dv SIGHUP
is ignored, as under
.Xr nohup 1 .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Copy a file line by line:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ lw < app.log > copy.log
.Ed
.Pp
Follow a log and keep only errors and warnings:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ tail \-F app.log | lw \-\-filter include,patterns=ERROR,patterns=WARN
.Ed
.Pp
Prefix each line with the time, the level found in it and the source:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ tail \-F app.log | lw \-\-format txt
.Ed
.Pp
Serve standard input as a live stream on the loopback address only, since
the
.Cm http
sink listens on every address by default, and read it from another terminal
as server-sent events; a client gets the lines that arrive while it is
connected:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ journalctl \-f | lw \-\-sink http,host=127.0.0.1,port=8080
$ curl \-N http://127.0.0.1:8080/stream
.Ed
.Pp
Follow every
.Pa .log
file in a directory, as
.Ql tail \-F
does, and write the new lines unparsed to one file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ lw \-\-source 'file,directory=/var/log/app,pattern=*.log,raw=true' \e
    > all.log
.Ed
.Pp
Follow every file in a directory, with the level names in color on a
terminal:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ lw \-\-preset tail,path=/var/log/app
.Ed
.Pp
Print the configuration a set of options makes, to keep as a file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ lw \-\-preset serve,path=/var/log/app,tls=self \-\-dump > serve.toml
.Ed
.Pp
Ship a directory from an edge to an aggregator: on the aggregator, create
the authority, its certificate and a user for the edge; copy
.Pa ca.crt
and the password file to the edge; then run both:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# lw tls ca \-\-dir /etc/logwisp/pki
# lw tls cert \-\-ca-dir /etc/logwisp/pki \-\-name agg.example.org \-\-server
# lw auth add-user \-\-credentials /etc/logwisp/users.toml \e
    \-\-user edge-01 \-\-password-file /root/edge-01.pass
agg# lw \-\-preset aggregator,tls=files,users=/etc/logwisp/users.toml,\e
cert=/etc/logwisp/pki/agg.example.org.crt,\e
key=/etc/logwisp/pki/agg.example.org.key,out=/var/log/edges
edge# lw \-\-preset edge,path=/var/log/app,to=agg.example.org:9000,\e
ca=/etc/logwisp/pki/ca.crt,user=edge-01,password_file=/root/edge-01.pass
.Ed
.Pp
Write the annotated configuration to the file
.Nm
reads without
.Fl c ,
edit it, then check it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ lw config init
$ lw \-\-check
.Ed
.Pp
Check a configuration without binding a port or touching a log file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ lw \-\-check \-c /etc/logwisp/logwisp.toml
.Ed
.Pp
Run a configuration in the foreground with debug logging:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ lw \-c /etc/logwisp/logwisp.toml \-\-logging.level=debug
.Ed
.Pp
Create a credentials file the service account can read, add a user,
letting
.Nm
generate the password into a file, then reload the server:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# install \-m 0600 \-o logwisp \-g logwisp \e
    /dev/null /etc/logwisp/users.toml
# lw auth add-user \-\-credentials /etc/logwisp/users.toml \e
    \-\-user alice \-\-password-file /root/alice.pass
# systemctl reload logwisp
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr systemd.exec 5 ,
.Xr sysusers.d 5 ,
.Xr tmpfiles.d 5 ,
.Xr daemon 8
.Pp
The guides to configuration, plugins, security and deployment, installed in
.Pa share/doc/logwisp
under the installation prefix and kept at
.Lk https://github.com/lixenwraith/logwisp/tree/main/doc
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
A listener without an
.Cm auth
block serves or accepts logs from anyone who can reach it.
Keep key, credentials and password files at mode 0600, owned by the account
.Nm
runs as; startup warns about world-readable ones.
